{
  "dom_id": "step-q159974",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5},{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. Given that the likelihood function of the sample $4.8,\\,0.7,\\,5.0,\\,2.7,\\,1.9,\\,2.7$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =4.8+0.7+5+2.7+1.9+2.7 \\\\  & =17.8 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(4.8)^{2}+(0.7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(2.7)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(2.7)^{2} \\\\  & =66.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(66.72-2(17.8)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-33.36\\,+\\,17.8\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-33.36+17.8\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$",
